A||||5||William Federle||Timing cover seal||||1||
Z||000000||William Federle||12-15-2000||08:22 PM||||I replaced the timing cover seal on the 2.5L engine in my 1984 Fiero. I removed the right rear wheel and since I couldn't hold the crankshaft immobile I used a Sears electric impact wrench to brake the pulley retaining bolt loose. Then I gently tapped the pulley and hub from the crank shaft.<P>The old seal was really worn - the rubber part didn't appear to have a lip pointing inward at an angle like the new one. I pulled the old seal with a seal puller and after lubing the new one with engine oil seated it. Then I replaced the pulley and hub, pulling it over the crank with the big bolt that holds it on, using the impact wrench again, since I wouldn't be able to torque it with a torque wrench. I checked for leaks after putting the alternator belt back on and starting the car. I couldn't see any leaks, so I drove the car around to get the IAC valve reset, and when I got back home checked again. No leaks so far.<p>[This message has been edited by William Federle (edited 12-15-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||ggarsk||12-16-2000||03:39 AM||||You are lucky...I've been fighting my seal. If your hub is worn to much,you have to put on a sleeve. I found that out after 3 seals. Keep your eye on itjust in case.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||William Federle||12-16-2000||06:51 AM||||Thanks for the tip. Where do you get the sleeve?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Carrolles||12-16-2000||07:10 AM||||I had the gasket between the timing chain cover and the block spring a leak on my 85se v-6.  What a joy to fix.  Pretty much the same procedure.<P>Last year I treated myself to a few goodies from Harbor Freight.  A bead blast cabinet, large pop rivet tool, and an electric impact wrench, universal puller set, and some small misc. tools.<P>The impact wrench is great.  I could not have pulled the harmonic balancer off without it.  And it's great for pulling wheels off.  No more having to break all the lug nuts loose while the car is on the ground then finishing while it is in the air.  I just put it on jack stands and zip zip zip and they're off.  Taking the wheels off makes many jobs easier due to accessability.<P>I visited a Harbor Freight store for the first time during the Thanksgiving Holidays in Baton Rouge, La.  They have just about everything you see in the catalog.<P>I hope everyone has a great Christmas and New Year.<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000004||Wolfhound||12-16-2000||07:20 AM||||Carroll, Montgomery Alabama now has a Harbor Freight store. Opened November first.<P> It,s located southwest of the intersection of Ware's Ferry Road and the Eastern Bypass.<BR>Coy||||reg||7
Z||000005||ggarsk||12-16-2000||03:06 PM||||You can get it at Shucks(Checker-Kragen)Auto Parts. It comes with the timing cover gasket kit. It's Fel-Pro with sleeve.||||reg||1
